<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/2016-09-08-unquote.jpg" alt="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boull%C3%A9e,_Etienne-Louis_-_Project_for_a_library.jpg" width="998" height="638" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-43932" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/2016-09-08-unquote.jpg 998w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/2016-09-08-unquote-300x192.jpg 300w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/2016-09-08-unquote-600x384.jpg 600w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 998px) 100vw, 998px" /></p>
<blockquote><p>
Meek young men grow up in libraries, believing it their duty to accept the views, which Cicero, which Locke, which Bacon, have given, forgetful that Cicero, Locke, and Bacon were only young men in libraries when they wrote these books.
</p></blockquote>
<p>&#8212; Emerson, <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_American_Scholar">&#8220;The American Scholar,&#8221;</a> 1838</p>
<p>(Daniel Dennett: “A scholar is just a library’s way of making another library.”)</p>
